 {"id": "CF-250", "title": "P2 — nothing on the control host reports seed drift: verify-node.sh has no seed leg, so a node whose prompts or skills were edited by hand still reports green", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-05 by the diag-node-1 tick while closing CF-220. The seed contract is real and it can go red on the node: `python3 $HOME/node_seed.py check --record $HOME/.hermes/seed-applied.json --home $HOME` printed `FAIL seed_check — prompts/foreman.md drifted (75da16fb93fd != 82c020b6f316)` and exit 1 after one line was appended to a seeded prompt, and `PASS seed_check — 13 files present and matching` after the file was restored (docs/audits/code-factory-cf220-2026-11-05.md). Nothing on the control host invokes it. `scripts/verify-node.sh` folds exactly one node-tree probe into VERIFY: — checkout_freshness, added by CF-244 — and grep for `seed` in verify-node.sh returns nothing; `scripts/project-node.sh` runs `node_seed.py build|apply` at provision time only. So the operator-visible verdict over a live node cannot see a hand-edited prompt or skill, and after provisioning deletes $HOME/node_seed.py and $HOME/seed.tar* the only copy of the checker on the node is gone until something stages it again. This is the same class CF-244 closed for the checkout: the instrument exists, the control-host leg does not.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. `scripts/verify-node.sh --agent <id> --server <host>` prints a `seed_drift` row: PASS naming the file count when the node's seeded tree matches its record, FAIL naming each drifted or absent path and the expected vs measured digest prefix when it does not. The row is produced by one `bunker exec` round trip, in the idiom of the CF-244 freshness leg (stage node_seed.py to a temp path, run `check`, remove it).\n2. A node with no `$HOME/.hermes/seed-applied.json` reports WARN naming the absent record and `--strict-seed` turns that into FAIL; exit-code semantics stay distinguishable from the freshness leg's (a drifted seed alone makes `VERIFY: FAIL`).\n3. `tests/selftest.sh` gains a section proving both directions offline, against a fixture node directory and a fixture bunker shim (no network): green arm PASS with the file count, red arm FAIL naming the drifted path, absent-record arm WARN then FAIL under `--strict-seed`. Each arm must be able to go the other way, per AGENTS.md rule 2.\n4. `specs/NODE.md` records the new leg in the same table cell that documents checkout_freshness, and `docs/ACCEPTANCE-PLAN.md` counts are updated to the measured selftest section count.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixing commit, and the row's closeout quotes the live command and output for one real node (cf-node on bunker-las-03), green and, with a single appended line, red.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:15:08Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:50:41Z", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "bd4c739833c8d13b0ebdd3ccaf8bcd5263083ec0", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "SKIP", "worker_summary": "seed_drift leg: control-host VERIFY now folds the node seed record; live green/red proven on cf-node", "foreman_note": "Closed at bd4c739 (judged verdict bdfb4ce9: Tier1 PASS + Tier2 PASS, bound to commit 3b2668e, which is bd4c739 plus a sibling board-only commit). AC1 live: scripts/node_seed_drift.py --agent cf-node --server bunker-las-03 printed \"  PASS  seed_drift — /home/bunker-cf-node/.hermes/seed-applied.json: 13 seeded files present and matching\" / \"RESULT: PASS\", real exit 0, from ONE bunker exec round trip; scripts/verify-node.sh --agent cf-node --server bunker-las-03 folded the row into \"VERIFY: FAIL (1)\" where the only FAIL was the pre-existing checkout_freshness drift (behind=4, CF-248 class), so the seed_drift PASS is a real row and not a short-circuit. AC2 live: appending one line to $HOME/.hermes/prompts/foreman.md on the node turned the run into \"  FAIL  seed_drift — ... 1 seeded path(s) differ from the record: prompts/foreman.md drifted (expected 82c020b6f316, measured 9d38e7a1c66b)\" with real_rc=1 (measured unpiped, not through a pipeline); restoring the file returned real_rc=0 and the 13-file PASS, so the live red arm and its restore are both measured, not asserted. The absent-record WARN path is proven offline, not on the live node (that node has a valid record and removing it would be a real mutation the row did not ask for). AC3: tests/selftest.sh section 48 runs 11 offline arms — green naming the file count, tamper naming the path and both digest prefixes, restore back to green, absent record WARN exit 3, the same fixture FAILing under --strict-seed exit 1, restore, a stub-bunker arm proving the staging is one exec round trip, and --json carrying record_present/file_count; bash tests/selftest.sh printed SELFTEST: PASS (308 PASS rows) at bd4c739. AC4: docs/audits/cf198-section-mapping.sh prints RESULT: OK with arms_plan=51 arms_tree=51, and specs/NODE.md records the leg in the checkout_freshness cell family. AC5 is the live pair quoted above. Residual: the node's checkout is 4 commits behind origin/main, reported by the freshness leg in the same run and untouched here. CI: gh run list --repo coding-hermes/code-factory returns [] (the repo carries no workflow runs), so ci=SKIP is unverified, not green.", "detail": {"evidence": [{"run_id":"bdfb4ce9","ts":"2026-11-05T13:50:41Z"}]}}
 {"id": "CF-251", "title": "cf-node is absent from the fleet repo-scope manifest, so every job it runs is refused a GitHub token", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1) fleet-repo-scope.json carries an entry for cf-node scoped to coding-hermes/code-factory only. 2) The dispatcher's job-boundary test stops printing 'no scoped token for cf-node' and proves a scoped token is issued. 3) A token issued for cf-node cannot reach any repo outside its declared scope - negative case asserted, not asserted in prose. 4) The manifest is validated at dispatch time so a missing agent is a named refusal, not a silent unauthenticated job.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:42:27Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:42:27Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "53ebae6e07a65534f1d764e70a6d79548a0048c9619ab6e479f79accf6a5f784"}}
 {"id": "CF-252", "title": "P0 - every foreman-class tick dies at router rating selection (unservable-rating, chain_length 0): 174 ticks over 155 lanes since 2026-11-04, and no lane holds the inferred long_horizon/tool_use/mechanical rungs", "status": "pending", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-05T13:50Z by the diag-node-1 tick, while checking CF-245's premise. Every foreman-class tick of the code-factory family dies before any model is considered, and the same refusal is fleet-wide: 174 ticks across 155 distinct lanes since 2026-11-04 carry failure_reason \"unservable-rating\" (sqlite3 over ~/.hermes/scheduler.db, error LIKE '%unservable-rating%'). The scheduler record for code-factory-2026-11-05-13-10-24 reads: gateway HTTP 503, {\"error\":\"no eligible model for this rating in the registry and no fallback lane could serve (requirements_unmet: the rating outruns the fleet — see TR-185)\",\"failure_reason\":\"unservable-rating\",\"lane_class\":\"primary\"}, and the embedded _router block shows chain_length 0, max_hops 3, ladder [], hops_attempted 0, terminal_reason \"no-hops\", served_by_reason \"unservable-rating\". The binding constraint is named in the same block: \"inferred-matrix clamp (P0_FORE): dropped: profile P0_FORE never declares long_horizon, and no lane holds long_horizon=3 (rungs [-4, -3, -2, 5])\" plus the same sentence for tool_use=3 and mechanical=0 — i.e. the classifier infers three dimensions at rungs that (a) the P0_FORE profile does not declare and (b) no registry lane holds, so the clamp cannot drop them and no hop is eligible. The requirement levels vary per prompt (agent_tick 2/3/4 observed) and the failing set includes lanes with no model pin at all (code-factory-duckbrain), so this is not a per-lane pin defect: CF-245's disabled pin is a separate, real defect that cannot by itself restore service. Registry evidence for the owner: /home/kara/task-router/registry.json tables.models[1036] is gpt-6.1-sol/openai-codex disabled:true (\"credits exhausted ... TR-341\"), and task_profile_requirements carries no P0_FORE row while tables.task_profiles does, which is why the profile declares no dimension. A second consequence: the code-factory family has produced no board work from scheduler ticks since 2026-11-04 even though the board holds 147 pending rows, so every lane's \"failed\" streak is this one fault, not 16 independent ones.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. A scheduler tick for any code-factory lane leaves a terminal non-503 status after the fix, proven by reading that tick's row in ~/.hermes/scheduler.db: the code-factory family shows at least one tick with status completed (or failed for a reason other than unservable-rating) and NO tick carrying the string \"unservable-rating\" in its error after the fix timestamp.\n2. One command re-measures the class and prints zero: a census over ~/.hermes/scheduler.db for ticks spawned after the fix whose error contains \"unservable-rating\" returns 0, and the same query run against the pre-fix window still returns the 174/155 figures, so the instrument is proven able to see both states.\n3. The remedy is named where it lives and is one of the two shapes the error's own text points at: either the P0_FORE profile declares the dimensions its prompts are inferred to need (long_horizon, tool_use, mechanical) so the clamp can drop an unsatisfiable rung, or the registry gains at least one lane holding the rungs the refusal names — plus a loud WARN-and-serve fallback when no lane holds an inferred rung, so an unservable rating degrades to a served lane instead of a 503 that costs the whole tick. Whichever shape is chosen, the row records the file and the exact edit.\n4. A regression test fails when a task profile leaves an inferred dimension with no satisfying lane rung: a fixture profile plus a fixture registry where the clamp must drop, asserting the router either serves a lane or refuses with a named reason that is not \"no eligible model\".\n5. The row states its ownership boundary: the files it changes live in the task-router repository (registry.json task_profile_requirements / the router's rating resolver), not this one, so AGENTS.md rule 1 is honoured by filing there rather than editing another repo's config from a code-factory tick.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:51:10Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:51:10Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9ae6faa857f5efe3d35aa9bcad6ee8967d0cac5c93c4cfab8ac5516a9538b3fe"}}
-{"id": "CF-253", "title": "The node harness raises its stream read timeout to 1800s, so a stalled model stalls the job for half an hour", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": null, "acceptance_criteria": "1. A stalled upstream produces an error and a non-zero exit within a bounded time, instead of a run that sits until an external kill.\n2. The bound is configurable and its effective value is logged at request start, so an operator can tell 'waiting' from 'hung'.\n3. A test simulates an upstream that accepts the connection and sends no bytes, and asserts the harness fails within the bound.\n4. The failure is recorded in the session store, not only on stdout, so a killed run still shows why.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:08:14Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T14:19:45Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "c0a6396c063b1a88bc20510da5b758f41fd7c15740870df9e49b8ef5574be673"}, "foreman_note": "Found by harness-level logging on cf-node (2026-11-04). Ran the real 4,079-byte directive under 'hermes chat -v' with a 150s cap; the verbose log shows the exact stall: '06:05:32 API Request - Model: hermes, Messages: 1, Tools: 18', 'Total message size: ~5,386 tokens', then 'agent.chat_completion_helpers - DEBUG - Local provider detected (http://100.97.236.14:8775/v1) — stream read timeout raised to 1800s', and NOTHING until '06:07:52 - Received signal 15' (140 seconds of silence, ended by the test cap). So the harness itself sets a 1800s stream read timeout for local providers — which is why the two real runs sat 1447s and 2342s. The upstream stall is the trigger; the 1800s default is why it looks like a hung job instead of a failed request. Same proxy answers 4.4KB/20KB/60KB and a streaming request in 3-6s from the control host, so the difference in the harness request is its 18-tool schema payload and the streaming tool-call path.\n\nRuled out (2026-11-04), each measured rather than assumed. (1) Payload size: 4.4KB 5.9s, 20KB 2.7s, 60KB 3.4s — all 200. (2) Streaming: 200, 180 chunks, 3.4s. (3) Tool schemas: stream+tools at 2 and 18 schemas returned first byte in 5.9s and 4.7s. (4) Large responses through the NODE's own harness: a 1200-word essay (10,800 bytes) rc=0 in under 150s. So neither size, streaming, tool schemas, nor long output triggers it. What the failing run shows is different: the verbose log stops at 'API Request - Model: hermes, Messages: 1, Tools: 18 ... ~5,386 tokens' and then produces NO first byte for 140s, so the stall is before the first response token for that request shape, not during a tool loop (no tool calls are ever logged and no child process ever appears). The remaining unknown is the exact request body the harness emits; capturing it is the next step, and the proxy's BrokenPipe at the same timestamp is the other end of the same event.\n\nExact request captured (2026-11-04). Added capture_request() to the proxy; it now appends the body a node actually sends to ~/.hermes/logs/cf-inference-requests.jsonl. Ran the real directive through the node harness with a 120s cap: rc=124, 36 bytes again, and the capture recorded exactly one request — 69,163 bytes, 2 messages, 18 tools, stream=true, keys [max_tokens, messages, model, stream, stream_options, tools]. That is 69KB against my largest successful probe of 60KB, and it is the first request shape carrying stream_options, which none of my reconstructions had. Replay of the captured body was blocked: the control-host replay returned 401, so the token I extracted from cf-node-tokens.json does not match the one the node is configured with — that mismatch is itself recorded here rather than worked around. Next step is a verbatim replay FROM the node using its own configured credential, with the capture cap raised from 20KB to the full body so nothing is truncated.\n\nVerbatim replay attempt, 2026-11-04 — and the upstream is now the suspect. Capture raised to the full body: the node's real request is 69,163 bytes (68,907 chars) with 2 messages, 18 tools, stream=true, stream_options present, and the capture is written on every attempt, which proves the proxy DOES read the whole 69KB from the node — the request arrives, so the stall is downstream of the proxy's body read. Replayed that exact body FROM THE NODE using the node's own credential (CF_PROXY_API_KEY in ~/.hermes/.env, never printed): urllib fails in 0.4s with [Errno 32] Broken pipe. Attempted the same replay from the control host and got 401 for every value in ~/.hermes/secrets/cf-node-tokens.json, while the node's own credential authenticates fine — so that file is not the authority the proxy honours and my host-side probes were never speaking as the node. Establishes two facts and one next step: (a) the node's request reaches and is fully read by the proxy; (b) the proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642, which is the remaining upstream and the only component not yet measured with this body. Next: measure :8642 directly with the same 69KB body using the gateway credential read at runtime from the hermes secret env, under the current host load, and record first-byte time; if it stalls there, the fault is the gateway under load, not the node, not the proxy, not the transport.\n\nROOT CAUSE LOCATED 2026-11-04, from the proxy's own usage log. The proxy's _forward() does urllib r.read() — it BUFFERS the whole upstream response before sending a single byte to the node, even when the client asked for stream=true. Measured consequence: one chat request logged '07:18:18 status=0 IncompleteRead secs=1289.38 model=hermes', and the maximum recorded chat duration is 2140.26s (35 minutes). So the sequence is: node sends 69KB (arrives, proven by capture) -> proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642 -> the gateway takes 20-35 minutes to produce this turn -> the proxy holds the socket and delivers nothing -> the node's harness waits with its 1800s stream timeout -> the job wall (1447s, 2342s) kills it first. Nothing is wrong with the node, the tailnet, the payload size, the tool schemas, or the proxy's admission: the node's inference is being served by a saturated control-host gateway, and every layer above it hides that fact. Two fixes now have names: (1) the proxy must pass SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering, so the harness sees progress and the 1800s wait becomes a real first-byte; (2) node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load — it needs its own lane or provider, since the whole point of CF-002 is that nodes think without keys, not that they think at the control host's pace.\n\nFIX 1 IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED 2026-11-04: the proxy now passes SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering the whole turn (_forward streams when the request body says stream=true, sends headers immediately, writes and flushes each 4096-byte chunk, and logs first_byte + streamed on the usage row). Verification: a node chat through the pass-through logged 'status=200 streamed=True first=0.0 secs=5.4 bytes=1354' and the node returned STREAM-OK, so the node now sees tokens as they are produced rather than after the whole generation. What this does NOT fix: the upstream gateway still takes 20-35 minutes to produce the foreman turn (measured 1289.38s and max 2140.26s before the change), so a real directive still exceeds its wall. It converts 'the node sees nothing for 1800s' into 'the node sees a live stream that is too slow' — which is the difference between an unattributable hang and a capacity problem with a number on it. Fix 2, still open: node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load."}
