s, stream=true, stream_options present, and the capture is written on every attempt, which proves the proxy DOES read the whole 69KB from the node — the request arrives, so the stall is downstream of the proxy's body read. Replayed that exact body FROM THE NODE using the node's own credential (CF_PROXY_API_KEY in ~/.hermes/.env, never printed): urllib fails in 0.4s with [Errno 32] Broken pipe. Attempted the same replay from the control host and got 401 for every value in ~/.hermes/secrets/cf-node-tokens.json, while the node's own credential authenticates fine — so that file is not the authority the proxy honours and my host-side probes were never speaking as the node. Establishes two facts and one next step: (a) the node's request reaches and is fully read by the proxy; (b) the proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642, which is the remaining upstream and the only component not yet measured with this body. Next: measure :8642 directly with the same 69KB body using the gateway credential read at runtime from the hermes secret env, under the current host load, and record first-byte time; if it stalls there, the fault is the gateway under load, not the node, not the proxy, not the transport.\n\nROOT CAUSE LOCATED 2026-11-04, from the proxy's own usage log. The proxy's _forward() does urllib r.read() — it BUFFERS the whole upstream response before sending a single byte to the node, even when the client asked for stream=true. Measured consequence: one chat request logged '07:18:18 status=0 IncompleteRead secs=1289.38 model=hermes', and the maximum recorded chat duration is 2140.26s (35 minutes). So the sequence is: node sends 69KB (arrives, proven by capture) -> proxy forwards to the control host's gateway at 127.0.0.1:8642 -> the gateway takes 20-35 minutes to produce this turn -> the proxy holds the socket and delivers nothing -> the node's harness waits with its 1800s stream timeout -> the job wall (1447s, 2342s) kills it first. Nothing is wrong with the node, the tailnet, the payload size, the tool schemas, or the proxy's admission: the node's inference is being served by a saturated control-host gateway, and every layer above it hides that fact. Two fixes now have names: (1) the proxy must pass SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering, so the harness sees progress and the 1800s wait becomes a real first-byte; (2) node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load — it needs its own lane or provider, since the whole point of CF-002 is that nodes think without keys, not that they think at the control host's pace.\n\nFIX 1 IMPLEMENTED AND VERIFIED 2026-11-04: the proxy now passes SSE through as it arrives instead of buffering the whole turn (_forward streams when the request body says stream=true, sends headers immediately, writes and flushes each 4096-byte chunk, and logs first_byte + streamed on the usage row). Verification: a node chat through the pass-through logged 'status=200 streamed=True first=0.0 secs=5.4 bytes=1354' and the node returned STREAM-OK, so the node now sees tokens as they are produced rather than after the whole generation. What this does NOT fix: the upstream gateway still takes 20-35 minutes to produce the foreman turn (measured 1289.38s and max 2140.26s before the change), so a real directive still exceeds its wall. It converts 'the node sees nothing for 1800s' into 'the node sees a live stream that is too slow' — which is the difference between an unattributable hang and a capacity problem with a number on it. Fix 2, still open: node inference must not queue behind the control host's own load.\n\nLANE FIX VERIFIED 2026-11-04, and it moved the failure forward. Repointed the proxy at a direct provider lane via a systemd drop-in (CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM/CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM_MODEL/CF_PROXY_UPSTREAM_KEY_ENV), key still held only on the control host so CF-002 holds. Measurements: same 4,046-byte directive now answers in 0.9s to first byte, 6.6s complete, against 1289-2140s through the control host's gateway. On the node, LANE-OK returns immediately and the real directive now EXECUTES TOOLS for the first time — 06:22:11 shows three skill_view calls (coding-hermes-map, coding-hermes-foreman, code-factory-foreman) within three seconds, where before it never left the first model call. New failure, now visible because the run is observable: mid-generation the stream stalls and the client retries, discarding buffered output — '06:26:01 Discarded stale stream chunk from attempt 1 (discarded_chunks=99 discarded_bytes=35363)', growing to 35KB of discarded bytes before the wall kills the run at rc=124. So the node is no longer starved of inference; it is losing long streams mid-flight and re-running the turn. Next: fix the stream survival (chunk framing / keepalive on the pass-through) and re-measure a full directive turn end to end."}
